-**`each` over a `ListCell` builds only the rows a write names: one push into 1,000 rows builds ONE row and reads ONE key, where a plain `SignalCell<List<T>>` re-reads every key and diffs the run.** A112 S3, the delta-driven `each`. Until now `each` answered every change with the whole-list pass — read every key, diff against the held run, apply the plan — even when the source already knew exactly what changed, because `ListCell` and `KeyedCell` record their writes as `SeqOp`s and nothing in `std::ui` read them. Placing an `Each` (and an `EachValues`) now asks its source one more thing, `DeltaFeed<T>`, a new `std::reactive` trait with two blankets: every `Source<List<T>>` answers "no log" (so every plain source keeps the pass, exactly as before, and nothing that placed before is refused), and every `DeltaSource<List<T>, SeqOp<T>>` answers with its log — the more specific blanket wins by B378's rule. On the op path a `Splice` cuts the rows that left and builds the rows that arrived before the next row's marker, a `SetAt` rebuilds that one row unless the key and the item are both unchanged (then it costs nothing, as the pass's `Keep` would), and a `Reset` (a wholesale `set`, or a cursor that fell behind the log) and a `Move` fall back to the pass — the `Move` at the list AFTER the move, not at `source.get()`, which is the state after every op the drain holds and would have left the ops after it applied to the wrong run (the patch held at Order 39 had exactly that bug; the walk below reds on it at turn 4). The bound sits on the `Slot` impls and not on `each` itself, so a source that is not a list source (an rpc mirror) is still refused with the one error that names `List<Todo>`. `each`'s row list is now its region's own list rather than a second cell kept in step (the `Shared` census drops one). `each_by` and the server twin keep the pass — the server twin renders once, so it has no change to follow. **Measured, callgrind Ir per push into a 1,000-row list under `node --jitless` (slope between 20 and 120 pushes; loadavg 14.3–15.0):** the plain `SignalCell` with `each` costs 52,485,214 Ir per push; the `ListCell` with `each` costs 6,297,816, of which 20,391 is `each`'s op path and 6,275,259 is the `ListCell` write itself — the deep copies M86 names (a bare `SignalCell` update costs 4,353, and a no-op subscriber on the `ListCell` adds 2,166, so the copies are in the write, not in the notification). Fixing M86 takes the op path to ~25 K Ir per push. Two pins in `ui_rows`: eleven edits over one `ListCell` with the tree AND the cost asserted per edit (`push` `cut=0 built=1 keys=1`, `remove` `cut=1 built=0 keys=0`, a `set_at` to an equal value costing nothing), the plain-`SignalCell` control keeping the pass (`keys=8` over four rows), and one push and one removal at 1,000 rows (`built=1 keys=1`, `cut=1 keys=0`) — red with the op path planted out (`keys=2002`); and S2's random walk extended THROUGH `each`: 300 turns of 1–4 ops from the whole vocabulary inside one `batch`, three runs mounted on the cell (`each`, `each` over a chained `map_each`, `each_values`), the document read back after every turn — red at turn 4, 1 and 13 under three plants (the `Move` fallback at `source.get()`, arrivals placed at the anchor, a key-only `SetAt` skipping its rebuild). Tracker A112 slice S3 (`proposal/incremental-collections.md` §9, §12).
